                           Process Management

--> Process is a program that is currently running in the system
--> Process is a program in execution
--> Process is a simply running instance of a program

*Types of process:
1. Foreground process
2. Background Process
3. Deamon Process

1. Foreground process:
--> A foreground process is a process that runs directly in your terminal and
occupies your terminal until it finishes.
(terminal is a place where we type commands and see their output
Shell accepts commands from user, interprets them and ask the OS to execute them)

2. Background Process:
--> Background processs runs without occupying your terminal.
--> use the & symbol to start a process in the background.

jobs command:
--> The jobs command in Linux is used to display the status of jobs running in the current shell session. It is mainly used for managing background processes.
syntax: jobs

ps command:
--> The ps command in Linux stands for Process Status. It is used to display information about currently running processes in the system.
--> System administrators use ps to monitor processes, check PIDs, identify resource-consuming processes, and troubleshoot applications.
syntax: ps
-->shows current terminal processes

ii] ps -ef
--> Shows all processes in full format

iii] ps aux
--> Shows detailed information about all processes

top command:
--> The top command in Linux is used to monitor running processes and system resource utilization in real time.
--> It displays CPU usage, memory usage, load average, running processes, and other system information.
syntax: top

kill commmand:
--> The kill command in Linux is used to terminate a process by sending a signal to its Process ID (PID).
--> Linux administrators use the kill command to stop unresponsive applications, terminate unwanted processes, and manage running services.
syntax: kill -9 <pid>
here,
-9 means Forcefully terminate process
pid means process ID

